# file-toto — Implementation (C11)

## Module layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `include/file_toto.h` | `FILE_TOTO_BUF_SIZE` (65536), `FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE` (512), `FILE_TOTO_DESC_CAP`, `FILE_TOTO_VERSION_STRING`, `FILE_TOTO_EXIT_*` enum, `file_toto_opts_t`, `classify_content()` (`static inline`) |
| `include/file_toto_emit.h` | `file_toto_emit_error()`, `file_toto_emit_bad_flag()` |
| `include/file_toto_magic.h` | `magic_entry_t` (signature at an offset from the start or from the end of the file) and `match_magic()` / text helpers used by `classify_content()` |
| `include/file_toto_identify.h` | `file_toto_run()` declaration |
| `include/file_toto_cli.h` | `parse_flags()`, `print_help()`, `print_version()` |
| `src/main.c` | `main()` only — dispatch help/version, parse flags, collect operands, call `file_toto_run()` |
| `src/file_toto_emit.c` | stderr diagnostics: `file-toto: <context>: <strerror(errno)>` |
| `src/file_toto_identify.c` | `file_toto_run()`, per-operand `stat`/`lstat`/`open`/`read`, format result line, `write()` to stdout |
| `src/file_toto_magic.c` | Compiled-in magic signatures and text/binary heuristics backing `classify_content()` |
| `src/file_toto_cli.c` | Flag parse, help/version text |

Each `.c` file opens with a **chain-of-thought comment block** (responsibility,
syscalls, heap policy, classify strategy, C standard). Preserve that pattern
when adding files.

## C standard

**ISO C11** (`-std=c11`), `-D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L` for Linux POSIX.1-2008
(`open`, `read`, `write`, `stat`, `lstat`, etc.).
MinGW builds define `_WIN32`; symlink/`lstat` and binary-mode opens are guarded
there (see below).

## Buffer size (64 KiB prefix)

`FILE_TOTO_BUF_SIZE` is **65536** bytes.

Rationale: matches real `file`'s default `encoding` limit (65K bytes examined
for text / encoding detection), covers every embedded magic signature, and
avoids reading whole files. It stays on the stack, well under the default
Linux (8 MiB) and Windows / MinGW (2 MiB) main-thread stack sizes. Real
`file`'s 1 MiB `bytes` limit is deliberately not matched: it would need a
non-stack buffer.

`FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE` is **512** bytes: the last bytes of the file, used only by
end-of-file magic entries. 512 is the size of the Apple DMG `koly` trailer, the
end-of-file signature in the table. Instead of reading more of the file,
accuracy comes from reading the right places: signatures at fixed offsets
inside the prefix, the PE header located through the pointer at `0x3C`, and
this small tail.

`FILE_TOTO_DESC_CAP` is the stack capacity for a single human-readable or MIME
description string (including NUL). Keep descriptions short; do not grow via heap.

## Classification order (`classify_content` / `file_toto_run`)

Mirror classic Unix `file(1)` test order — **first match wins**:

1. **Filesystem tests** (`stat` / `lstat`): directory, symlink, fifo, socket,
   block/char device, empty regular file, missing/unreadable (result line).
2. **Magic tests**: table entries whose signature sits at an offset from the
   start of the file (checked in the prefix) or from the end (checked in the
   tail), in table order; then the PE header check (`MZ` + pointer at `0x3C`
   + `PE\0\0`), which falls back to MS-DOS executable.
3. **Language / content heuristics**: ASCII/UTF-8 text vs “data” / binary.

`classify_content()` is the pure, testable core for steps 2–3 (and empty /
zero-length content). Filesystem-only outcomes are decided in
`file_toto_identify.c` before calling it.

## Per-file path (`src/file_toto_identify.c`)

```
for each operand:
  lstat/stat according to -h / -L
  if missing/unreadable:
    if opts.fatal_errors (-E): emit stderr, exit_status = 1, continue (or stop
      only if policy requires — default: continue, then exit 1)
    else: write stdout result line "<name>: cannot open …", keep exit 0
  if non-regular (dir, symlink without -L, etc.): write type from mode; continue
  open + read up to FILE_TOTO_BUF_SIZE (binary mode on Windows)
  tail: if the prefix reached EOF, the last FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE bytes of buf
        (no extra I/O); else one lseek to FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE before the end
        + read into a stack tail buffer. Any failure => no tail (not an error)
  classify_content(buf, n, tail, tail_len, …) → description
  write result line to stdout
```

**Default output:** `filename: description\n`  
**`-b` / `--brief`:** `description\n` only  
**`-i` / `--mime`:** MIME string instead of human description (e.g. `text/plain`,
`application/pdf`, `inode/directory`)

## `classify_content()` contract

In `include/file_toto.h` (or thin wrappers declared there, defined via magic
header):

- **Signature:** `classify_content(buf, len, tail, tail_len, mime, out, out_cap)`
- **Precondition:** `buf` non-NULL if `len > 0`; `out` non-NULL; `out_cap >= 1`
- **`tail` / `tail_len`:** the last `tail_len` bytes of the file (at most
  `FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE`); may point into `buf`; `NULL` / `0` when unknown, which
  simply disables end-of-file entries
- **Postcondition:** `out` holds a NUL-terminated description; return `0` on
  success, `-1` only if `out_cap` is too small (callers must size
  `FILE_TOTO_DESC_CAP` adequately — treat undersize as a programming error)
- **`len == 0`:** empty content → `"empty"` (human) or appropriate MIME
- Pure with respect to I/O: **no** `open`/`read`/`write` inside
  `classify_content()` — only examine the provided bytes and option flags

## Hot path rules

**Allowed:**

- Stack `unsigned char buf[FILE_TOTO_BUF_SIZE]`
- Stack `unsigned char tail_buf[FILE_TOTO_TAIL_SIZE]`
- One `read()` (or short read loop to fill the prefix) per regular file
- At most one extra `lseek()` + read for the tail, only when the prefix did not
  reach EOF
- `write(STDOUT_FILENO, …)` for each result line with partial-write / `EINTR` retry
- Table-driven magic matching over the prefix

**Forbidden in classify / identify:**

- Linking **libmagic** or shipping/parsing external magic files
- `malloc` / `calloc` / `realloc` on the per-file path
- `printf` / `fprintf` / `puts` / `fputs` / `fwrite` for result lines

## Symlinks (`-h` / `-L`)

| Flag | Behaviour |
|------|-----------|
| default / `-h` / `--no-dereference` | `lstat`; report symlink as symlink (do not follow) |
| `-L` / `--dereference` | `stat` / follow; classify the target |

On platforms without useful symlink semantics under `_WIN32`, document the
guarded fallback in code comments; do not invent fake symlink types.

## Binary mode (Windows)

| Platform | Behaviour |
|----------|-----------|
| **Linux / POSIX** | No-op — `read()` returns raw bytes |
| **Windows / MinGW (`_WIN32`)** | After `open()`, set `_O_BINARY` so magic bytes are not corrupted by CRLF translation |

## Signals

Unlike `yes-toto`, `file-toto` has a **finite** per-operand loop:

- No custom `SIGINT` handler — default Ctrl+C is correct.
- No `SIGPIPE` ignore required for correctness of a short result write; if a
  write hits `EPIPE`, treat as a write failure (exit `1`) after optional
  `EINVAL`→`EPIPE` normalisation on Windows pipes.

## Exit codes

| Code | Constant | Meaning |
|------|----------|---------|
| `0` | `FILE_TOTO_EXIT_OK` | Classification finished; `--help`; `--version`; missing/unreadable files **without** `-E` (POSIX) |
| `1` | `FILE_TOTO_EXIT_ERR` | Bad flag, write failure, signal/setup failure if any, or filesystem errors when `-E` is set |

## Error messages

Stderr diagnostics (true errors / `-E`):

`file-toto: <context>: <strerror(errno)>`

Invalid option:

`file-toto: invalid option -- '<flag>'`

Never print those to stdout. Classification / cannot-open **result** lines are
stdout only.

## Argument parsing (`src/file_toto_cli.c`)

1. Scan **all** `argv[1…]`: exact `--help` or `--h` → help + exit 0 (**wins**
   over version).
2. Else exact `--version` or `--v` → `file-toto <version>\n` + exit 0.
3. Parse flags left to right; `--` ends flag parsing.
4. Supported short/long options (see `project-overview.mdc`): `-b`/`--brief`,
   `-i`/`--mime`/`--mime-type`, `-h`/`--no-dereference`, `-L`/`--dereference`,
   `-E`. Combined shorts (e.g. `-bi`) allowed.
5. Remaining non-flag tokens are file operands. **At least one** operand is
   required (no stdin-as-default in the baseline — matches common `file`
   usage; do not invent `-` stdin classification unless explicitly requested).
6. Unknown flag → emit bad-flag message + exit 1.

## Magic table policy

- Compiled into `file_toto_magic.c` — **libc only**, no external DB.
- Prefer longest / most specific match when multiple signatures could apply.
- Keep the table small and documented; expand only when asked.
- Each `magic_entry_t` has `offset` + `from_end`: `from_end == 0` means the
  signature starts `offset` bytes after the start of the file (must lie inside
  the prefix); `from_end == 1` means it starts `offset` bytes before the end
  (must lie inside the tail).
- Current table (in order): PNG, GIF89a, GIF87a, JPEG, ELF, PDF, ZIP (all at 0);
  ISO Media `ftyp` at 4; POSIX tar `ustar` at 257; ISO 9660 `CD001` at 32769;
  Apple DMG `koly` at 512 from the end.
- PE is not a table entry: `match_pe()` runs after the table, reads the
  little-endian `e_lfanew` at `0x3C`, and requires `PE\0\0` there (PE32 /
  PE32+ from the optional-header magic); a bare `MZ` is MS-DOS executable.

## Naming

Public run entry point: **`file_toto_run()`** (declared in
`include/file_toto_identify.h`, defined in `src/file_toto_identify.c`).
